title the shape and the bytes -- the roster digest learns to read content, and finds a second fault on its first run
prompt The HUSH seat prompt: roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, rota row N mod 5, QA on touch, read scope, ListAgents before a file moves, claim-as-override when my lane's queue is empty. My own last recommend named the door: the digest red, proven in a pen and left unbooked.
think LISTAGENTS FIRST, and it answered none reachable -- yet a process listing named a peer the tool cannot see: Dream's Codex loop held a --hot pass in ~/grain-dream, its own checkout, five minutes into my open. Separate tree, lawful concurrency by the lock's own design (%359), and no file of mine. Later a second peer appeared the only way that one can: xy moved twice under me, and the second move had touched all three files I was editing.
think ROTA ROW 4 BY N=3844 MOD 5 -- Earth, the concrete row, whose question is what already stands true before anyone argues with it. The threshold, the one-clock law, TWO_ROOMS, and the marked value. It read as the lap's own instruction: my whole round is one reading -- a digest -- standing in for a fact it did not contain.
think THE DIGEST RED, PROVEN FOUR WAYS BEFORE A LINE CHANGED. tree_digest hashes `git rev-parse HEAD` plus `git status --porcelain`. Porcelain prints a status letter and a path and NOTHING ELSE, so a file already carrying `M` reads `M path` however often its bytes change. In a throwaway repository: an unstaged-modified file rewritten, an untracked file rewritten, a staged file re-staged, and a staged-and-modified file rewritten ALL return a character-identical digest. The last lap named the first; the other three are this lap's, and the sharpest is the staged one -- `--hot` is exactly the pass a round runs over its own staged paths, so a forty-minute hot close could print tree_moved=no over content it never read.
think THE REPAIR, AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT CHANGED IT MIDWAY. First draft hashed each untracked file with sha256sum in a shell loop. Measuring rather than shipping it: at 2,003 untracked files that loop took 10,393ms where `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard | git hash-object --stdin-paths` took 45ms -- 231x, one process rather than thousands, and the unbounded-fork hazard gone with it. The final reading is HEAD, porcelain, `git diff HEAD --binary` for tracked content (the index's own blob hashes standing in before a first commit), and hash-object for the untracked. 261ms per call against the elder 148ms on 15,165 tracked files.
think THE SECOND FAULT, FOUND BY THE SHARPER READING ON ITS FIRST RUN. Four control cases went red. The runner appends its own hit-ledger row BETWEEN its two digests, so the runner is one of the things its own tree_moved reading measures. The field gitignores that shelf so the growth was invisible; a pen does not. The run card and the evidence room already keep the discipline and say why beside themselves -- the digest describes the tree the GUARDS saw. The ledger simply predated it. Its write waits for the close now, and the probe writes its own row where it exits, since nothing can move between a probe's open reading and its return.
think LOAD-BEARING, PROVEN BY RUNNING THE SAME CONTROL AGAINST THE ELDER RUNNER. Eleven new readings: one showing porcelain standing still across the very edit, six refusing, four free. Against the elder runner: `no` to all six refusals, `yes` to all four welcomes. So the new asserts catch what the old could not and refuse nothing an ordinary dirty round does.
obs THE COLD PASS CAME BACK 103 RUN, 99 GREEN, 4 RED, AND THAT PREEMPTED MY LAP. `readme_metrics` had drifted -- the front door read fascia 64 where a fresh measurement reads 68, so the most-read page this project owns was under-reporting the tree's own health; regenerated. `reds_pin_capacity` read unrecorded_shelves=63 over a ceiling of 62 -- the %364/%371 shelf had landed without its recital line; I wrote one, then found Sound had written the same line upstream from the same reading, so I dropped mine and took theirs. `remember_git_nib` closes with this commit's own nib. `rule_twin` is gate %7 and stays.
obs THE GATE, MEASURED RATHER THAN RESTATED. rule_twin reds at drift 38 against a ceiling of 36, and the ceiling has stood at 36 since 20260828. Checking out both rule directories at that commit and running the same scan: 36 then, 38 now -- `gauge-style` healed, and THREE vocabulary rules seated 20260828 have drifted since, `vocabulary-dependent`, `vocabulary-first-resident`, and `vocabulary-red-over-bug`. The classifier calls all three two_way, so gate %7's middle door opens for none of them. The two-way drift class is still GROWING rather than merely standing, which is the part the card did not say.
obs FETCH-BEFORE-BOOK EARNED ITS PLACE TWICE IN ONE ROUND. My row was drafted as %372; the fetch before booking returned next_free=376, four rows having landed upstream in the interval. The same fetch showed the peer had edited all three files I had open. I stashed, fast-forwarded, popped -- clean, no conflict -- and then checked what mattered rather than trusting it: both hands' marks stand in all three files, and my diff removes exactly ten lines, every one of them mine.
obs THE CARD IS FULL, AND THAT IS A FINDING. construction/ITINERARY.md stood at 24,567 of its 24,576 bytes at HEAD -- nine bytes of room. Every sentence I added had to be paid for by one removed, and I spent six passes trimming my own prose to land at 24,573. A living pin with nine bytes of headroom means the next lap writes nothing or condenses first. Named here rather than taken: a condense touches other lanes' live lines, and two of them are mid-round.
obs REDS BOOKED %380 AND FOLDED TO MAKE ROOM. The pin had 3,172 bytes of headroom and my first row wanted 4,444 -- three times the 1,411-byte median, which was my prose rather than the ledger's problem. I tightened it to 3,149 and then folded %361-%363 to a new shelf anyway, because booking a row that leaves 21 bytes for the next one is not room. %360 takes a repaired-further clause and stays OPEN: its remainder is 21 red guards in lanes I do not hold and the pier clones' untracked publisher copies at Keaton's hand.
obs THE REBASE BROUGHT A RED WITH IT, AND I CLOSED IT RATHER THAN PUSHING PAST IT. xy moved twice under this round -- once while I read, once while the two-hour hot pass ran -- and the second move carried three broken rows into session-logs/date/README-index-20260830.md: two logs linked by bare filename from a shelf one directory up, and a duplicate row for 20260830.193650 at the wrong depth. readme_reach reds on a living broken link, and reds_fold cascades off it, so two rostered guards stood red for every body in the fleet over three link prefixes. Repaired here: the paths take their 20260830/ room and the duplicate goes. Then the SECOND rebase brought NINE more of the same shape, from several hands within one evening, so this is a pattern rather than a slip: a shelf index sits in session-logs/date/ and its logs sit one directory deeper, and a row written by hand keeps forgetting the prefix. readme_reach catches it, and only inside a two-hour roster pass, which is why nine could accumulate between passes. The cheap loom is a commit-time reading of the day shelf's own links -- named here, not taken. Not my rows, and not a lane boundary either: a shared index in my own room, and the guard that names it is the instrument I hold.
